{"duration_api_ms":93375,"stop_reason":"tool_use","session_id":"dee4cb8c-0720-403b-9ddb-eefb7e0ee06e","total_cost_usd":0.3658714,"usage":{"input_tokens":8,"cache_creation_input_tokens":54233,"cache_read_input_tokens":120407,"output_tokens":13632,"output_tokens_details":{"thinking_tokens":4881},"server_tool_use":{"web_search_requests":0,"web_fetch_requests":0},"service_tier":"standard","cache_creation":{"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":54233,"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0},"inference_geo":"global","iterations":[{"input_tokens":2,"output_tokens":10459,"cache_read_input_tokens":58234,"cache_creation_input_tokens":4455,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":4455},"type":"message"}],"speed":"standard","fallback_credit":null},"modelUsage":{"claude-haiku-5-5":{"inputTokens":5472,"outputTokens":31,"cacheReadInputTokens":0,"cacheCreationInputTokens":0,"webSearchRequests":0,"costUSD":0.0005627000000000001,"contextWindow":1000000,"maxOutputTokens":128000,"thinkingTokens":0,"cacheCreationEphemeral5mInputTokens":0,"cacheCreationEphemeral1hInputTokens":0,"canonicalModel":"claude-haiku-5-5","provider":"firstParty","costBasis":"list"},"claude-sonnet-5-5":{"inputTokens":8,"outputTokens":13632,"cacheReadInputTokens":120407,"cacheCreationInputTokens":54233,"webSearchRequests":0,"costUSD":0.3653087,"contextWindow":1000000,"maxOutputTokens":128000,"thinkingTokens":4881,"cacheCreationEphemeral5mInputTokens":0,"cacheCreationEphemeral1hInputTokens":54233,"canonicalModel":"claude-sonnet-5-5","provider":"firstParty","costBasis":"list"}},"permission_denials":[],"terminal_reason":"completed","fast_mode_state":"off","fast_mode_disabled_reason":"sdk_opt_in_required","subagent_stats":{"spawned":0,"requested":{"background":0,"foreground":0,"unset":0},"started_in_background":0,"max_depth":0,"spawned_by_subagents":0,"completed":0,"failed":0,"killed":{"parent":0,"user":0,"system":0},"refused":{"depth_limit":0,"concurrency_limit":0,"budget":0},"by_type":{}},"safety_stops":0,"is_error":false,"num_turns":6,"subtype":"success","api_error_status":null,"result":"{\"reviewer\":\"power_multiple_testing_auditor\",\"paper_id\":\"bicycle-infrastructure-paper\",\"run_status\":\"ok\",\"summary\":\"The paper is a measurement and pipeline-validation study with no estimated treatment effects, no preregistration and no formal hypothesis tests, so most of this remit is absent. Four interpretation risks remain. (1) The abstract and conclusion state that human–LLM agreement matched human–human agreement, but the pilot has only three relevant articles. Section 4 concedes that the two agreement rates cannot be tested and are indicative only, but the abstract and conclusion drop that caveat. (2) The full-run headline counts of 92 articles, 43 projects and 29 dated projects rest on a validation with three positives, so recall and precision on the rare relevant class are essentially uncalibrated. (3) The seasonal clustering of openings is described as indicated by unreported, untested shares of relevant articles. (4) The proposed housing-listing design lets an increase in any mention type count as support, with no declared primary outcome or multiplicity handling. The parser-quality output was read. Narrative text pages were used as evidence, and the structured tables were not relied on.\",\"findings\":[{\"id\":\"POWER-001\",\"category\":\"null_effect_overinterpreted\",\"finding_summary\":\"The abstract and conclusion state that human–LLM agreement on relevance equals or 'matched' human–human agreement, a claim of equivalence resting on only three relevant articles. The caveat that the sample is too small to compare the two rates appears only in Sections 4 and 8.\",\"issue_type\":\"manuscript_issue\",\"severity\":\"medium\",\"confidence\":\"medium\",\"location\":{\"page\":1,\"page_label\":\"1\",\"section\":\"Abstract\",\"text_quote\":\"the relevance of 97 percent of the articles, the same rate as between two human coders,\",\"precision\":\"exact\"},\"claim_text\":\"On the pilot, the LLM agrees with a human coder on the relevance of 97 percent of the articles, the same rate as between two human coders, and gives the same opening date for all three relevant articles.\",\"assessment\":\"partially\",\"cannot_verify_reason\":null,\"evidence_summary\":\"Tables 3 and 4 each show 33 of 34 agreements (3 relevant/relevant, 30 not relevant/not relevant, 1 disagreement). Sections 4 and 8 openly say that, with 3 relevant articles, the sample cannot test whether the two agreement rates differ and that results are indicative only. The abstract and conclusion nonetheless say the rates are 'the same' and 'matched' and do not carry that qualification. Of the 34 articles, 30 are agreed negatives, so overall agreement is dominated by the easy majority class and carries little information on agreement for the relevant class. An exact binomial interval for 3 of 3 successes is very wide (lower two-sided 95% bound about 0.29; this is my illustration, not a figure from the paper). The date comparison, with the LLM matching the pilot coder on 3 of 3 against human–human agreement on 2 of 3, is likewise too small to rank the LLM against a human coder. No uncertainty interval or equivalence margin is reported. The paper does not state this as a null effect, but 'same rate' functions as an equivalence claim without power or equivalence calibration. The main-text caveat is a real mitigation, which is why severity is medium rather than high.\",\"source_objects\":[{\"id\":\"S1\",\"type\":\"text\",\"label\":\"Abstract, page 1\",\"path\":\"work/bicycle-infrastructure-paper/parsed/pages/page_001.md\",\"page\":1,\"page_label\":\"1\",\"section\":\"Abstract\",\"text_quote\":\"the relevance of 97 percent of the articles, the same rate as between two human coders,\",\"url\":null,\"object_id\":\"OBJ-ae6010b8e427194ea28d441e\",\"correspondence\":\"text\",\"verification_note\":\"Abstract equivalence wording located in the page text.\"},{\"id\":\"S2\",\"type\":\"text\",\"label\":\"Section 4 caveat, page 7\",\"path\":\"work/bicycle-infrastructure-paper/parsed/pages/page_007.md\",\"page\":7,\"page_label\":\"7\",\"section\":\"4 Validation\",\"text_quote\":\"test whether the two agreement rates differ. The results are thus indicative only.\",\"url\":null,\"object_id\":\"OBJ-4e6da301f188ae5c7e5d0a63\",\"correspondence\":\"text\",\"verification_note\":\"The authors' own limitation, in the sentence following the footnote marker.\"},{\"id\":\"S3\",\"type\":\"text\",\"label\":\"Conclusion, page 12\",\"path\":\"work/bicycle-infrastructure-paper/parsed/pages/page_012.md\",\"page\":12,\"page_label\":\"12\",\"section\":\"7 Conclusion\",\"text_quote\":\"human–LLM agreement on relevance matched human–human agreement\",\"url\":null,\"object_id\":\"OBJ-a6c6d31e7be45f25b775aa47\",\"correspondence\":\"text\",\"verification_note\":\"Conclusion repeats the equivalence framing without the caveat.\"}],\"claim_evidence_links\":[{\"claim_text\":\"LLM agreement with the human coder is 'the same rate' as between two human coders\",\"source_object_ids\":[\"S1\",\"S3\",\"S2\"],\"relation\":\"partial\",\"note\":\"Point estimates coincide at 33 of 34, but the authors state the sample cannot test the difference; the abstract and conclusion omit this caveat.\"}],\"numeric_check\":null,\"suggested_fix\":\"State in the abstract and conclusion that the pilot has only three relevant articles and that the comparison is descriptive. Report agreement on the relevant class (for example positive agreement or kappa) with exact confidence intervals. Replace 'the same rate' and 'matched' with 'similar in this small pilot'.\"},{\"id\":\"POWER-002\",\"category\":\"power_claim_unsupported\",\"finding_summary\":\"The full-run headline counts (92 relevant articles, 43 projects, 29 dated to day or month) are reported as results although validation covers only 34 pilot articles with three positives. The pilot cannot detect modest false-negative or false-positive rates on the rare relevant class, and the day/month dating of the 29 projects was validated on only three articles.\",\"issue_type\":\"manuscript_issue\",\"severity\":\"low\",\"confidence\":\"medium\",\"location\":{\"page\":12,\"page_label\":\"12\",\"section\":\"7 Conclusion\",\"text_quote\":\"The pipeline was applied to 3,187 articles, identifying\",\"precision\":\"exact\"},\"claim_text\":\"The pipeline was applied to 3,187 articles, identifying 43 unique projects. Of these, 29 can be dated to the day or month.\",\"assessment\":\"partially\",\"cannot_verify_reason\":null,\"evidence_summary\":\"Section 5 reports 92 relevant articles out of 3,187, a relevance rate under 3%. The validation set has 34 articles, 3 relevant by the pilot coder and 4 by the LLM, so it contains almost no information on recall (missed openings) or on precision at the 3,187-article scale. Date accuracy was checked for three articles. The full run used rounds 2–4, a different period from the pilot, and the prompt was revised after the first run. Section 8 acknowledges that 'a random sample from the full run of 3,187 articles should also be checked against human coding', so the limitation is disclosed. The abstract and conclusion nevertheless present the counts without a calibration or error bound. Because the project count is the paper's main output, readers cannot tell how many openings were missed or how many dated projects are correct. The paper makes no claim that no further openings exist. Section 5 does state that 'All counties except Østfold are represented'. This is descriptive, but without recall calibration it should not be read as absence of projects in Østfold.\",\"source_objects\":[{\"id\":\"S1\",\"type\":\"text\",\"label\":\"Conclusion, page 12\",\"path\":\"work/bicycle-infrastructure-paper/parsed/pages/page_012.md\",\"page\":12,\"page_label\":\"12\",\"section\":\"7 Conclusion\",\"text_quote\":\"The pipeline was applied to 3,187 articles, identifying\",\"url\":null,\"object_id\":\"OBJ-a6c6d31e7be45f25b775aa47\",\"correspondence\":\"text\",\"verification_note\":\"Headline counts presented in the conclusion.\"},{\"id\":\"S2\",\"type\":\"text\",\"label\":\"Next steps, page 12\",\"path\":\"work/bicycle-infrastructure-paper/parsed/pages/page_012.md\",\"page\":12,\"page_label\":\"12\",\"section\":\"8 Next Steps\",\"text_quote\":\"a random sample from the full run of 3,187 articles should also be checked against\",\"url\":null,\"object_id\":\"OBJ-a6c6d31e7be45f25b775aa47\",\"correspondence\":\"text\",\"verification_note\":\"Authors acknowledge that no full-run validation exists.\"},{\"id\":\"S3\",\"type\":\"text\",\"label\":\"Table 4 comparison, page 8\",\"path\":\"work/bicycle-infrastructure-paper/parsed/pages/page_008.md\",\"page\":8,\"page_label\":\"8\",\"section\":\"4.2 Agreement between human and LLM\",\"text_quote\":\"The LLM gives the same date as the pilot coder in all three.\",\"url\":null,\"object_id\":\"OBJ-b41cc221a4f064e5990acabf\",\"correspondence\":\"text\",\"verification_note\":\"Date validation rests on three articles.\"}],\"claim_evidence_links\":[{\"claim_text\":\"Full-run counts of openings and dated projects are reported as pipeline output\",\"source_object_ids\":[\"S1\",\"S3\",\"S2\"],\"relation\":\"partial\",\"note\":\"Validation is limited to three positive pilot articles, and the full-run accuracy check is deferred.\"}],\"numeric_check\":null,\"suggested_fix\":\"In the abstract and results, label the counts as unvalidated pipeline output. Alternatively, add a random-sample check of the full run (including a sample of articles coded not relevant) and report precision, recall and date accuracy with confidence intervals.\"},{\"id\":\"POWER-003\",\"category\":\"exploratory_result_overemphasized\",\"finding_summary\":\"Section 5 states that the monthly pattern in the share of relevant articles 'indicates' that openings cluster before winter and summer holidays. No shares, counts or tests are shown, and the authors themselves note that the archive has few articles from July–August 2026.\",\"issue_type\":\"manuscript_issue\",\"severity\":\"low\",\"confidence\":\"medium\",\"location\":{\"page\":10,\"page_label\":\"10\",\"section\":\"5 Results\",\"text_quote\":\"This indicates that openings cluster before winter and summer\",\"precision\":\"exact\"},\"claim_text\":\"October–December 2025 and May–June 2026 have the highest share of relevant articles, and February–April 2026 the lowest. This indicates that openings cluster before winter and summer holidays.\",\"assessment\":\"partially\",\"cannot_verify_reason\":null,\"evidence_summary\":\"The seasonal inference rests on an unreported month-by-month share of relevant articles among 92 relevant articles across roughly 13 months, so each cell is small. The share of relevant articles measures article coverage, not openings, and 92 articles map to 43 projects, so reporting intensity and project clustering are confounded. The authors flag the thin July–August coverage, which is a mitigation. No table, figure or interval is provided, and there is no stated prior hypothesis, so the pattern is exploratory. Even so, the verb 'indicates' presents it as a finding. The paper does not rely on this pattern for later claims, so the issue is low severity.\",\"source_objects\":[{\"id\":\"S1\",\"type\":\"text\",\"label\":\"Section 5 seasonal sentence, page 10\",\"path\":\"work/bicycle-infrastructure-paper/parsed/pages/page_010.md\",\"page\":10,\"page_label\":\"10\",\"section\":\"5 Results\",\"text_quote\":\"This indicates that openings cluster before winter and summer\",\"url\":null,\"object_id\":\"OBJ-e9b90e0f75ce05ff1ce150cc\",\"correspondence\":\"text\",\"verification_note\":\"Claim sits directly below Table 6, which contains no monthly breakdown.\"}],\"claim_evidence_links\":[{\"claim_text\":\"Openings cluster before winter and summer holidays\",\"source_object_ids\":[\"S1\"],\"relation\":\"partial\",\"note\":\"Table 6 reports only totals and date precision; the monthly shares behind the claim are not displayed, and the claim concerns openings although the unit is articles.\"}],\"numeric_check\":null,\"suggested_fix\":\"Report the monthly counts and shares (or a figure) and label the pattern as a descriptive, exploratory observation. Alternatively, rephrase to 'is consistent with' and base it on project-level opening dates rather than article shares.\"},{\"id\":\"POWER-004\",\"category\":\"outcome_family_unclear\",\"finding_summary\":\"The proposed housing-listing analysis treats an increase in any type of cycling mention as evidence that a route is a stronger selling point. Model (2) lists the outcome as 'any type, or each type separately; alternatively, its price', so the primary outcome, the family of outcomes and the multiplicity handling are not declared.\",\"issue_type\":\"manuscript_issue\",\"severity\":\"low\",\"confidence\":\"medium\",\"location\":{\"page\":10,\"page_label\":\"10\",\"section\":\"6.1 An application to house listing data\",\"text_quote\":\"An increase in any type of mention would thus indicate that the route has made\",\"precision\":\"exact\"},\"claim_text\":\"An increase in any type of mention would thus indicate that the route has made cycling a stronger selling point.\",\"assessment\":\"partially\",\"cannot_verify_reason\":null,\"evidence_summary\":\"No effects are estimated; Section 6.3 says the specifications are examples, and Section 6.1 states that the listings have not yet been linked to the database. The risk is in the proposed inference rule. Appendix A defines six mention types plus an 'any mention' indicator (Table 8), and Section 6.3 allows each type and price as outcomes in model (2), with two hypotheses (θ > 0 and δ > 0). Treating a rise in any one of these as support gives a union decision rule with no declared primary outcome and no adjustment, and so inflates false positives. Conversely, 'any mention' is dominated by non-infrastructure mentions such as proximity and parking. No pre-specified primary outcome (for example the infrastructure category or the any-mention indicator) is named. The paper also says nothing about power: the sample is 44 projects, of which only 29 are dated to day or month, and the design clusters listings by project. Because this is a plan, not a result, the issue is a design-stage interpretation risk and severity is low.\",\"source_objects\":[{\"id\":\"S1\",\"type\":\"text\",\"label\":\"Section 6.1 decision rule, page 10\",\"path\":\"work/bicycle-infrastructure-paper/parsed/pages/page_010.md\",\"page\":10,\"page_label\":\"10\",\"section\":\"6.1 An application to house listing data\",\"text_quote\":\"An increase in any type of mention would thus indicate that the route has made\",\"url\":null,\"object_id\":\"OBJ-e9b90e0f75ce05ff1ce150cc\",\"correspondence\":\"text\",\"verification_note\":\"Inference rule located in the text.\"},{\"id\":\"S2\",\"type\":\"text\",\"label\":\"Model (2) outcome definition, page 11\",\"path\":\"work/bicycle-infrastructure-paper/parsed/pages/page_011.md\",\"page\":11,\"page_label\":\"11\",\"section\":\"6.3 Proposed econometric analysis\",\"text_quote\":\"type in Appendix A separately; alternatively, its price)\",\"url\":null,\"object_id\":\"OBJ-95b2618a59737e0b1725c4df\",\"correspondence\":\"text\",\"verification_note\":\"Outcome list for model (2); the formula and its subscripts were not relied on.\"},{\"id\":\"S3\",\"type\":\"text\",\"label\":\"Hypotheses for model (2), page 11\",\"path\":\"work/bicycle-infrastructure-paper/parsed/pages/page_011.md\",\"page\":11,\"page_label\":\"11\",\"section\":\"6.3 Proposed econometric analysis\",\"text_quote\":\"The hypotheses are θ > 0 and δ > 0: openings increase mentions of cycling nearby, and\",\"url\":null,\"object_id\":\"OBJ-95b2618a59737e0b1725c4df\",\"correspondence\":\"text\",\"verification_note\":\"Two directional hypotheses stated for a multi-outcome design.\"}],\"claim_evidence_links\":[{\"claim_text\":\"A rise in any mention type indicates a stronger selling point\",\"source_object_ids\":[\"S1\",\"S2\",\"S3\"],\"relation\":\"partial\",\"note\":\"The union rule over several outcome types, with price as an alternative outcome, lacks a declared primary outcome or multiplicity plan.\"}],\"numeric_check\":null,\"suggested_fix\":\"Before estimation, state a primary outcome (for example the any-mention indicator or the infrastructure category). Label the other mention types and price as secondary or exploratory, or specify a multiplicity adjustment or an aggregate (index) test. Add a short power or minimum-detectable-effect discussion based on the number of projects and listings near them.\"}],\"notes\":[\"The paper contains no preregistration, no estimated treatment effects and no formal significance tests. Power, outcome-family and multiplicity checks therefore applied only to the validation comparisons, the descriptive claims and the proposed econometric design.\",\"The attention-versus-date-precision comparison (17 of 18 versus 2 of 26 projects) is explicitly hedged ('suggests') and offered as motivation for a proposed model, so it was not raised as a finding.\",\"Parser-quality output was read. Narrative page text was used as the evidence, and structured table CSV/markdown was not relied on. The Table 3 and 4 counts were read from the page text, which matches the parser auditor's image comparison.\"],\"completion\":{\"version\":1,\"state\":\"complete\",\"scope_description\":\"Full read of the 18-page parsed manuscript (full_text.md) for power or minimum-detectable-effect claims, outcome families, multiplicity, exploratory-versus-confirmatory status and strong null or equivalence language. Source IDs were matched against source_registry.json.\",\"checks_completed\":[\"Searched the abstract, main text and appendices for power, MDE, sample-size and preregistration statements. None found beyond the authors' acknowledgment that the pilot is too small.\",\"Assessed the human–human versus human–LLM agreement equivalence framing against Tables 3 and 4 and the caveats in Sections 4 and 8.\",\"Checked whether the full-run counts are supported by the validation scale.\",\"Checked the descriptive claims (seasonality, county ranking, listing trends) for inferential language.\",\"Checked the proposed models (1) and (2) for declared outcomes, hypotheses and multiplicity.\",\"Checked for subgroup, multi-arm and specification-search structure. None present.\"],\"missing_checks\":[],\"evidence_limitations\":[\"Table CSV/markdown objects are unreliable per the parser-quality output, so table values were read from the page text.\",\"Figure 1 and Figure 3 values were not read from images, and no finding depends on them.\",\"The pilot coding was done on 34 articles, and the full-run validation data are not in the retained artifacts, so error rates cannot be recomputed.\"]},\"coverage\":[{\"item_id\":\"assigned_remit\",\"source_object_ids\":[],\"claim\":null,\"check_performed\":\"Read the full parsed text and assessed power or MDE claims, outcome families, multiplicity, exploratory status and null or equivalence language across main text and appendices.\",\"status\":\"linked_finding\",\"reason\":\"Four low-to-medium interpretation risks found: the equivalence framing on three positives, the uncalibrated full-run counts, the seasonality claim, and the undeclared outcome family in the proposed design. No preregistration or inferential test structure exists beyond these. Scope was exhaustive over the text.\",\"finding_ids\":[\"POWER-001\",\"POWER-002\",\"POWER-003\",\"POWER-004\"],\"attempted\":true,\"importance\":\"central\",\"scope\":\"exhaustive\"},{\"item_id\":\"claim:attention-date-precision\",\"source_object_ids\":[\"OBJ-95b2618a59737e0b1725c4df\"],\"claim\":\"Of the 44 projects, 18 have an official opening ceremony, of which 17 are dated to the day, versus 2 of 26 without; this suggests that date precision depends on attention.\",\"check_performed\":\"Checked whether the descriptive contrast is presented as confirmatory or relied on for a substantive claim.\",\"status\":\"checked_no_issue\",\"reason\":\"The contrast is hedged ('suggests') and is used only to motivate a proposed test in model (1). No confirmatory or multiplicity-sensitive claim rests on it.\",\"finding_ids\":[],\"attempted\":true,\"importance\":\"peripheral\",\"scope\":\"exhaustive\"},{\"item_id\":\"claim:appendix-descriptive-listings\",\"source_object_ids\":[\"OBJ-6402cd90a906f1f82672a86f\",\"OBJ-e4efa6f7c9c10daa107ce8a0\"],\"claim\":\"The share of listings mentioning cycling rose by about 9 percentage points and varies across counties (Appendix A).\",\"check_performed\":\"Checked that the appendix trend and county comparisons are descriptive and make no inferential or null-effect claims.\",\"status\":\"checked_no_issue\",\"reason\":\"The appendix is labelled descriptive, reports no tests, and the 2024 gap is disclosed. No multiplicity or power issue arises.\",\"finding_ids\":[],\"attempted\":true,\"importance\":\"peripheral\",\"scope\":\"exhaustive\"}]}","structured_output":{"reviewer":"power_multiple_testing_auditor","paper_id":"bicycle-infrastructure-paper","run_status":"ok","summary":"The paper is a measurement and pipeline-validation study with no estimated treatment effects, no preregistration and no formal hypothesis tests, so most of this remit is absent. Four interpretation risks remain. (1) The abstract and conclusion state that human–LLM agreement matched human–human agreement, but the pilot has only three relevant articles. Section 4 concedes that the two agreement rates cannot be tested and are indicative only, but the abstract and conclusion drop that caveat. (2) The full-run headline counts of 92 articles, 43 projects and 29 dated projects rest on a validation with three positives, so recall and precision on the rare relevant class are essentially uncalibrated. (3) The seasonal clustering of openings is described as indicated by unreported, untested shares of relevant articles. (4) The proposed housing-listing design lets an increase in any mention type count as support, with no declared primary outcome or multiplicity handling. The parser-quality output was read. Narrative text pages were used as evidence, and the structured tables were not relied on.","findings":[{"id":"POWER-001","category":"null_effect_overinterpreted","finding_summary":"The abstract and conclusion state that human–LLM agreement on relevance equals or 'matched' human–human agreement, a claim of equivalence resting on only three relevant articles. The caveat that the sample is too small to compare the two rates appears only in Sections 4 and 8.","issue_type":"manuscript_issue","severity":"medium","confidence":"medium","location":{"page":1,"page_label":"1","section":"Abstract","text_quote":"the relevance of 97 percent of the articles, the same rate as between two human coders,","precision":"exact"},"claim_text":"On the pilot, the LLM agrees with a human coder on the relevance of 97 percent of the articles, the same rate as between two human coders, and gives the same opening date for all three relevant articles.","assessment":"partially","cannot_verify_reason":null,"evidence_summary":"Tables 3 and 4 each show 33 of 34 agreements (3 relevant/relevant, 30 not relevant/not relevant, 1 disagreement). Sections 4 and 8 openly say that, with 3 relevant articles, the sample cannot test whether the two agreement rates differ and that results are indicative only. The abstract and conclusion nonetheless say the rates are 'the same' and 'matched' and do not carry that qualification. Of the 34 articles, 30 are agreed negatives, so overall agreement is dominated by the easy majority class and carries little information on agreement for the relevant class. An exact binomial interval for 3 of 3 successes is very wide (lower two-sided 95% bound about 0.29; this is my illustration, not a figure from the paper). The date comparison, with the LLM matching the pilot coder on 3 of 3 against human–human agreement on 2 of 3, is likewise too small to rank the LLM against a human coder. No uncertainty interval or equivalence margin is reported. The paper does not state this as a null effect, but 'same rate' functions as an equivalence claim without power or equivalence calibration. The main-text caveat is a real mitigation, which is why severity is medium rather than high.","source_objects":[{"id":"S1","type":"text","label":"Abstract, page 1","path":"work/bicycle-infrastructure-paper/parsed/pages/page_001.md","page":1,"page_label":"1","section":"Abstract","text_quote":"the relevance of 97 percent of the articles, the same rate as between two human coders,","url":null,"object_id":"OBJ-ae6010b8e427194ea28d441e","correspondence":"text","verification_note":"Abstract equivalence wording located in the page text."},{"id":"S2","type":"text","label":"Section 4 caveat, page 7","path":"work/bicycle-infrastructure-paper/parsed/pages/page_007.md","page":7,"page_label":"7","section":"4 Validation","text_quote":"test whether the two agreement rates differ. The results are thus indicative only.","url":null,"object_id":"OBJ-4e6da301f188ae5c7e5d0a63","correspondence":"text","verification_note":"The authors' own limitation, in the sentence following the footnote marker."},{"id":"S3","type":"text","label":"Conclusion, page 12","path":"work/bicycle-infrastructure-paper/parsed/pages/page_012.md","page":12,"page_label":"12","section":"7 Conclusion","text_quote":"human–LLM agreement on relevance matched human–human agreement","url":null,"object_id":"OBJ-a6c6d31e7be45f25b775aa47","correspondence":"text","verification_note":"Conclusion repeats the equivalence framing without the caveat."}],"claim_evidence_links":[{"claim_text":"LLM agreement with the human coder is 'the same rate' as between two human coders","source_object_ids":["S1","S3","S2"],"relation":"partial","note":"Point estimates coincide at 33 of 34, but the authors state the sample cannot test the difference; the abstract and conclusion omit this caveat."}],"numeric_check":null,"suggested_fix":"State in the abstract and conclusion that the pilot has only three relevant articles and that the comparison is descriptive. Report agreement on the relevant class (for example positive agreement or kappa) with exact confidence intervals. Replace 'the same rate' and 'matched' with 'similar in this small pilot'."},{"id":"POWER-002","category":"power_claim_unsupported","finding_summary":"The full-run headline counts (92 relevant articles, 43 projects, 29 dated to day or month) are reported as results although validation covers only 34 pilot articles with three positives. The pilot cannot detect modest false-negative or false-positive rates on the rare relevant class, and the day/month dating of the 29 projects was validated on only three articles.","issue_type":"manuscript_issue","severity":"low","confidence":"medium","location":{"page":12,"page_label":"12","section":"7 Conclusion","text_quote":"The pipeline was applied to 3,187 articles, identifying","precision":"exact"},"claim_text":"The pipeline was applied to 3,187 articles, identifying 43 unique projects. Of these, 29 can be dated to the day or month.","assessment":"partially","cannot_verify_reason":null,"evidence_summary":"Section 5 reports 92 relevant articles out of 3,187, a relevance rate under 3%. The validation set has 34 articles, 3 relevant by the pilot coder and 4 by the LLM, so it contains almost no information on recall (missed openings) or on precision at the 3,187-article scale. Date accuracy was checked for three articles. The full run used rounds 2–4, a different period from the pilot, and the prompt was revised after the first run. Section 8 acknowledges that 'a random sample from the full run of 3,187 articles should also be checked against human coding', so the limitation is disclosed. The abstract and conclusion nevertheless present the counts without a calibration or error bound. Because the project count is the paper's main output, readers cannot tell how many openings were missed or how many dated projects are correct. The paper makes no claim that no further openings exist. Section 5 does state that 'All counties except Østfold are represented'. This is descriptive, but without recall calibration it should not be read as absence of projects in Østfold.","source_objects":[{"id":"S1","type":"text","label":"Conclusion, page 12","path":"work/bicycle-infrastructure-paper/parsed/pages/page_012.md","page":12,"page_label":"12","section":"7 Conclusion","text_quote":"The pipeline was applied to 3,187 articles, identifying","url":null,"object_id":"OBJ-a6c6d31e7be45f25b775aa47","correspondence":"text","verification_note":"Headline counts presented in the conclusion."},{"id":"S2","type":"text","label":"Next steps, page 12","path":"work/bicycle-infrastructure-paper/parsed/pages/page_012.md","page":12,"page_label":"12","section":"8 Next Steps","text_quote":"a random sample from the full run of 3,187 articles should also be checked against","url":null,"object_id":"OBJ-a6c6d31e7be45f25b775aa47","correspondence":"text","verification_note":"Authors acknowledge that no full-run validation exists."},{"id":"S3","type":"text","label":"Table 4 comparison, page 8","path":"work/bicycle-infrastructure-paper/parsed/pages/page_008.md","page":8,"page_label":"8","section":"4.2 Agreement between human and LLM","text_quote":"The LLM gives the same date as the pilot coder in all three.","url":null,"object_id":"OBJ-b41cc221a4f064e5990acabf","correspondence":"text","verification_note":"Date validation rests on three articles."}],"claim_evidence_links":[{"claim_text":"Full-run counts of openings and dated projects are reported as pipeline output","source_object_ids":["S1","S3","S2"],"relation":"partial","note":"Validation is limited to three positive pilot articles, and the full-run accuracy check is deferred."}],"numeric_check":null,"suggested_fix":"In the abstract and results, label the counts as unvalidated pipeline output. Alternatively, add a random-sample check of the full run (including a sample of articles coded not relevant) and report precision, recall and date accuracy with confidence intervals."},{"id":"POWER-003","category":"exploratory_result_overemphasized","finding_summary":"Section 5 states that the monthly pattern in the share of relevant articles 'indicates' that openings cluster before winter and summer holidays. No shares, counts or tests are shown, and the authors themselves note that the archive has few articles from July–August 2026.","issue_type":"manuscript_issue","severity":"low","confidence":"medium","location":{"page":10,"page_label":"10","section":"5 Results","text_quote":"This indicates that openings cluster before winter and summer","precision":"exact"},"claim_text":"October–December 2025 and May–June 2026 have the highest share of relevant articles, and February–April 2026 the lowest. This indicates that openings cluster before winter and summer holidays.","assessment":"partially","cannot_verify_reason":null,"evidence_summary":"The seasonal inference rests on an unreported month-by-month share of relevant articles among 92 relevant articles across roughly 13 months, so each cell is small. The share of relevant articles measures article coverage, not openings, and 92 articles map to 43 projects, so reporting intensity and project clustering are confounded. The authors flag the thin July–August coverage, which is a mitigation. No table, figure or interval is provided, and there is no stated prior hypothesis, so the pattern is exploratory. Even so, the verb 'indicates' presents it as a finding. The paper does not rely on this pattern for later claims, so the issue is low severity.","source_objects":[{"id":"S1","type":"text","label":"Section 5 seasonal sentence, page 10","path":"work/bicycle-infrastructure-paper/parsed/pages/page_010.md","page":10,"page_label":"10","section":"5 Results","text_quote":"This indicates that openings cluster before winter and summer","url":null,"object_id":"OBJ-e9b90e0f75ce05ff1ce150cc","correspondence":"text","verification_note":"Claim sits directly below Table 6, which contains no monthly breakdown."}],"claim_evidence_links":[{"claim_text":"Openings cluster before winter and summer holidays","source_object_ids":["S1"],"relation":"partial","note":"Table 6 reports only totals and date precision; the monthly shares behind the claim are not displayed, and the claim concerns openings although the unit is articles."}],"numeric_check":null,"suggested_fix":"Report the monthly counts and shares (or a figure) and label the pattern as a descriptive, exploratory observation. Alternatively, rephrase to 'is consistent with' and base it on project-level opening dates rather than article shares."},{"id":"POWER-004","category":"outcome_family_unclear","finding_summary":"The proposed housing-listing analysis treats an increase in any type of cycling mention as evidence that a route is a stronger selling point. Model (2) lists the outcome as 'any type, or each type separately; alternatively, its price', so the primary outcome, the family of outcomes and the multiplicity handling are not declared.","issue_type":"manuscript_issue","severity":"low","confidence":"medium","location":{"page":10,"page_label":"10","section":"6.1 An application to house listing data","text_quote":"An increase in any type of mention would thus indicate that the route has made","precision":"exact"},"claim_text":"An increase in any type of mention would thus indicate that the route has made cycling a stronger selling point.","assessment":"partially","cannot_verify_reason":null,"evidence_summary":"No effects are estimated; Section 6.3 says the specifications are examples, and Section 6.1 states that the listings have not yet been linked to the database. The risk is in the proposed inference rule. Appendix A defines six mention types plus an 'any mention' indicator (Table 8), and Section 6.3 allows each type and price as outcomes in model (2), with two hypotheses (θ > 0 and δ > 0). Treating a rise in any one of these as support gives a union decision rule with no declared primary outcome and no adjustment, and so inflates false positives. Conversely, 'any mention' is dominated by non-infrastructure mentions such as proximity and parking. No pre-specified primary outcome (for example the infrastructure category or the any-mention indicator) is named. The paper also says nothing about power: the sample is 44 projects, of which only 29 are dated to day or month, and the design clusters listings by project. Because this is a plan, not a result, the issue is a design-stage interpretation risk and severity is low.","source_objects":[{"id":"S1","type":"text","label":"Section 6.1 decision rule, page 10","path":"work/bicycle-infrastructure-paper/parsed/pages/page_010.md","page":10,"page_label":"10","section":"6.1 An application to house listing data","text_quote":"An increase in any type of mention would thus indicate that the route has made","url":null,"object_id":"OBJ-e9b90e0f75ce05ff1ce150cc","correspondence":"text","verification_note":"Inference rule located in the text."},{"id":"S2","type":"text","label":"Model (2) outcome definition, page 11","path":"work/bicycle-infrastructure-paper/parsed/pages/page_011.md","page":11,"page_label":"11","section":"6.3 Proposed econometric analysis","text_quote":"type in Appendix A separately; alternatively, its price)","url":null,"object_id":"OBJ-95b2618a59737e0b1725c4df","correspondence":"text","verification_note":"Outcome list for model (2); the formula and its subscripts were not relied on."},{"id":"S3","type":"text","label":"Hypotheses for model (2), page 11","path":"work/bicycle-infrastructure-paper/parsed/pages/page_011.md","page":11,"page_label":"11","section":"6.3 Proposed econometric analysis","text_quote":"The hypotheses are θ > 0 and δ > 0: openings increase mentions of cycling nearby, and","url":null,"object_id":"OBJ-95b2618a59737e0b1725c4df","correspondence":"text","verification_note":"Two directional hypotheses stated for a multi-outcome design."}],"claim_evidence_links":[{"claim_text":"A rise in any mention type indicates a stronger selling point","source_object_ids":["S1","S2","S3"],"relation":"partial","note":"The union rule over several outcome types, with price as an alternative outcome, lacks a declared primary outcome or multiplicity plan."}],"numeric_check":null,"suggested_fix":"Before estimation, state a primary outcome (for example the any-mention indicator or the infrastructure category). Label the other mention types and price as secondary or exploratory, or specify a multiplicity adjustment or an aggregate (index) test. Add a short power or minimum-detectable-effect discussion based on the number of projects and listings near them."}],"notes":["The paper contains no preregistration, no estimated treatment effects and no formal significance tests. Power, outcome-family and multiplicity checks therefore applied only to the validation comparisons, the descriptive claims and the proposed econometric design.","The attention-versus-date-precision comparison (17 of 18 versus 2 of 26 projects) is explicitly hedged ('suggests') and offered as motivation for a proposed model, so it was not raised as a finding.","Parser-quality output was read. Narrative page text was used as the evidence, and structured table CSV/markdown was not relied on. The Table 3 and 4 counts were read from the page text, which matches the parser auditor's image comparison."],"completion":{"version":1,"state":"complete","scope_description":"Full read of the 18-page parsed manuscript (full_text.md) for power or minimum-detectable-effect claims, outcome families, multiplicity, exploratory-versus-confirmatory status and strong null or equivalence language. Source IDs were matched against source_registry.json.","checks_completed":["Searched the abstract, main text and appendices for power, MDE, sample-size and preregistration statements. None found beyond the authors' acknowledgment that the pilot is too small.","Assessed the human–human versus human–LLM agreement equivalence framing against Tables 3 and 4 and the caveats in Sections 4 and 8.","Checked whether the full-run counts are supported by the validation scale.","Checked the descriptive claims (seasonality, county ranking, listing trends) for inferential language.","Checked the proposed models (1) and (2) for declared outcomes, hypotheses and multiplicity.","Checked for subgroup, multi-arm and specification-search structure. None present."],"missing_checks":[],"evidence_limitations":["Table CSV/markdown objects are unreliable per the parser-quality output, so table values were read from the page text.","Figure 1 and Figure 3 values were not read from images, and no finding depends on them.","The pilot coding was done on 34 articles, and the full-run validation data are not in the retained artifacts, so error rates cannot be recomputed."]},"coverage":[{"item_id":"assigned_remit","source_object_ids":[],"claim":null,"check_performed":"Read the full parsed text and assessed power or MDE claims, outcome families, multiplicity, exploratory status and null or equivalence language across main text and appendices.","status":"linked_finding","reason":"Four low-to-medium interpretation risks found: the equivalence framing on three positives, the uncalibrated full-run counts, the seasonality claim, and the undeclared outcome family in the proposed design. No preregistration or inferential test structure exists beyond these. Scope was exhaustive over the text.","finding_ids":["POWER-001","POWER-002","POWER-003","POWER-004"],"attempted":true,"importance":"central","scope":"exhaustive"},{"item_id":"claim:attention-date-precision","source_object_ids":["OBJ-95b2618a59737e0b1725c4df"],"claim":"Of the 44 projects, 18 have an official opening ceremony, of which 17 are dated to the day, versus 2 of 26 without; this suggests that date precision depends on attention.","check_performed":"Checked whether the descriptive contrast is presented as confirmatory or relied on for a substantive claim.","status":"checked_no_issue","reason":"The contrast is hedged ('suggests') and is used only to motivate a proposed test in model (1). No confirmatory or multiplicity-sensitive claim rests on it.","finding_ids":[],"attempted":true,"importance":"peripheral","scope":"exhaustive"},{"item_id":"claim:appendix-descriptive-listings","source_object_ids":["OBJ-6402cd90a906f1f82672a86f","OBJ-e4efa6f7c9c10daa107ce8a0"],"claim":"The share of listings mentioning cycling rose by about 9 percentage points and varies across counties (Appendix A).","check_performed":"Checked that the appendix trend and county comparisons are descriptive and make no inferential or null-effect claims.","status":"checked_no_issue","reason":"The appendix is labelled descriptive, reports no tests, and the 2024 gap is disclosed. No multiplicity or power issue arises.","finding_ids":[],"attempted":true,"importance":"peripheral","scope":"exhaustive"}]},"ttft_ms":2244,"type":"result","duration_ms":93441,"uuid":"ab31bfe3-802f-4f76-b549-f53034112ebe","ttft_stream_ms":1510,"time_to_request_ms":158,"first_content_frame_ms":1511,"queued_turn_count":0,"result_index":0,"credential_expires_at":"2026-12-08T17:18:28.888Z"}
